Subject: Size a field whose type bindgen has only a declaration of from what clang measured

A field is written as the Rust type codegen writes for the type it resolves to,
and the struct holding it is padded out around the width of that type. Where
bindgen holds only a declaration of the type, what codegen writes is an empty
struct - a `_unused: [u8; 0]` and nothing else - so the field covers no bytes
whatever the target gave the member. The struct is then laid out as though it
did: the tracker counts the bytes the member's offset reports, pads the struct
out to the size clang measured, and emits a type short of the object C++ builds
in it. A caller who asks C++ to construct one in storage sized from the Rust
type gets it built past the end.

C++ has no such member: a class has to be complete to declare one of. bindgen
does, because a nested class of a class template's instantiation reaches
`CompInfo::from_ty` with no definition cursor to read - `location` is what says
whether the declaration it was reached through was a definition, and there is
none - so `Beta<int>::iterator` is recorded as a declaration even though clang
instantiated it and measured it. `Beta<int>::Cursor` holds one of those, and a
class holding a `Cursor` came to nothing at all against the byte C++ gives an
empty class.

So take the route a field of an instantiation whose width nothing establishes
already takes: the width of what is written is unestablished, and the field is
written as a blob of the layout clang measured, which is that layout by
construction. Where clang measured nothing either - a declaration bindgen holds
because the header only declared the class, which nothing can be a member of -
the field is left as it was.
--- a/codegen/mod.rs
+++ b/codegen/mod.rs
@@ -1760,14 +1760,20 @@
 /// whose size is that struct's and not the one clang measured for the
 /// instantiation.
 ///
-/// Where nothing establishes how wide that type would be *and* nothing measured
-/// it either - which is an instantiation written as a definition clang did not
-/// instantiate - the field is written as a blob of the layout clang measured
-/// instead, which is that layout by construction. Leaving it as its own type
-/// would leave the struct holding it short of the object C++ builds there,
-/// silently: the tracker would count the bytes the target gave the member, pad
-/// the struct out as if the field had covered them, and emit a type narrower
-/// than the run it believed was filled.
+/// Where nothing establishes how wide that type would be, the field is written
+/// as a blob of the layout clang measured instead, which is that layout by
+/// construction. Leaving it as its own type would leave the struct holding it
+/// short of the object C++ builds there, silently: the tracker would count the
+/// bytes the target gave the member, pad the struct out as if the field had
+/// covered them, and emit a type narrower than the run it believed was filled.
+/// Two shapes reach that. One is an instantiation written as a definition clang
+/// did not instantiate. The other is a member whose type bindgen holds only a
+/// declaration of, which it writes as an empty struct: a class C++ cannot
+/// declare a member of, but bindgen has only a declaration of a nested class of
+/// a class template's instantiation, which C++ can. `Beta<int>::Cursor` holding
+/// a `Beta<int>::iterator` was written as a struct holding an empty one, and a
+/// class holding *it* came to nothing at all against the byte C++ gives an
+/// empty class.
 fn field_representation_layout(
     ctx: &BindgenContext,
     item: &Item,
@@ -1846,6 +1852,13 @@
         TypeKind::TemplateInstantiation(ref inst) => {
             instantiation_width(ctx, item, inst, measuring)
         }
+        // A declaration is written as an empty struct and holds nothing, which
+        // is not the width of the object C++ declared wherever clang measured
+        // one - and clang measures one wherever it has the definition this
+        // does not.
+        TypeKind::Comp(ref comp) if comp.is_forward_declaration() => {
+            WrittenWidth::Unestablished
+        }
         _ => WrittenWidth::AsMeasured,
     };
     measuring.remove(&item.id());
